  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "claim_5f5bd2ab3aaf475696efe81d2baa52d9",
      "statement": "Write protection rests on a single symmetric HS256 shared secret: the API verifies Authorization: Bearer tokens against the instance's JWT_SECRET and treats the token's `sub` claim as the caller's identity, with no identity provider, roles, scopes, or revocation in the loop. Any client holding a token signed with that secret can act as any user id, and tokens are minted out-of-band by the operator (docs/API.md documents a jsonwebtoken.sign snippet) rather than by any API endpoint.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://apps/api/src/middleware/auth.ts#L8-L49",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:10ce49ec72b60a626ef9b359a530abd08a29bb9c57c97debfe83bacb38a3aabe:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/API.md#L21-L57",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:5228e7a5554d0eb95cfdb23efc2cd3ea91da1af46357ac57bee4f60a8ab48aa1:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/OPENAPI.yaml#L408-L413",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:a81f88e81630e84bdcf62fe4ca371e36283fde4ffb35216db72a92e001eca32b:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_456a0d7f05834d2ebacd1db6bb759aff",
      "statement": "requireAuth has exactly three outcomes: a missing Authorization header or one not starting with 'Bearer ' returns 401 UNAUTHORIZED; a jwt.verify failure (bad signature, malformed, expired exp) returns 401 TOKEN_INVALID with the underlying cause swallowed so clients cannot tell expiry from a bad signature; success sets req.userId = decoded.sub and req.userEmail = decoded.email before calling next(). It performs no lookup of `sub` in user_profiles and no scope check.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://apps/api/src/middleware/auth.ts#L19-L49",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:c654293f6d94c4f8e1404a254611bf3239dc953d343d000b15b5330048ab574c:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/API.md#L56-L57",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:1f9f098c582f25f41d079aaad828a66432434a031a31aec9519b272a27f2213a:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_02624221b1204cf5843b3300b01aabb8",
      "statement": "JWT_SECRET is read once at module load into a module-level const with the code fallback 'dev-secret-change-in-production', so rotating the secret requires a process restart. No composition refuses to start when the variable is unset: docker-compose.prod.yml injects ${JWT_SECRET:-please-change-me} and boots normally on that placeholder, while docker-compose.yml and docker-compose.dev.yml never set JWT_SECRET at all (their .env.dev carries only NODE_ENV). The result is three divergent effective secrets - please-change-me, dev-secret-change-in-production, and .env.example's change-me-in-production - so a token that validates against one default-configured server is rejected by another.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://.env.dev#L1-L3",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:bd8a64700a8bb1582e257ed50ecb649431f4165671a380e32050e01add020a9a:"
